      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat ghee in a small pan. Add mustard seeds. When they pop, add curry leaves, garlic, ginger, and green chilli. Fry 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the tempered spices into the mashed potato with turmeric, coriander, lemon juice, and salt. Divide into 8 equal balls."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the batter: whisk chickpea flour, remaining turmeric, chilli powder, a pinch of salt, and enough water to make a thick coating batter."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil to 175 C in a deep pan. Dip each potato ball into the batter and lower into the oil. Fry 3 to 4 minutes until golden."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain on paper towels. Tuck each vada into a soft roll with green chutney and tamarind chutney. Serve immediately."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Batter slides off the potato ball",
              "cause": "Batter too thin or potato surface too wet",
              "fix": "Thicken the batter with more chickpea flour. Pat potato balls dry before dipping."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Coating is greasy",
              "cause": "Oil was not hot enough",
              "fix": "Test with a small drop of batter. It should rise to the surface immediately. Increase heat and wait before frying the next batch."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Vada pav is sometimes called Mumbai's burger. It was invented near Dadar railway station to feed factory workers who needed something cheap, filling, and fast to eat between shifts. Today it is sold at carts, canteens, and sit-down restaurants across the city. A proper vada pav is served with three chutneys: a dry garlic-coconut powder, a wet green coriander chutney, and a sweet tamarind chutney."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
